  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nThis afternoon...Temperatures range from a few degrees higher to a\nfew degrees lower than at his time yesterday. There was a weak\ncoastal eddy last night and low clouds from a shallow marine layer\nare persisting at the beaches of San Diego County. High resolution\nmodels indicate that a weak coastal eddy will likely return\ntonight.\n\nA shallow marine layer will persist through the forecast period\nwith night and morning low clouds mostly restricted to the coastal\nareas and western portions of the San Diego County valleys.\n\nThe large-scale pattern will remain quite stable, with high\npressure centered over the southern Rockies/Desert Southwest and\nlow pressure centered over the Gulf of Alaska. Through Wednesday,\nthe high pressure over SoCal will weaken slightly in response to a\nvigorous shortwave trough moving inland to our north. The\nresulting height falls and weak cyclonic flow aloft will allow the\nmarine layer to deepen a little and the low clouds to spread a\nlittle farther inland during the nights and mornings. Temperatures\nwill be generally lower across the region but will be more\nnoticeable nearer to the coast. By Wednesday, temperatures will\nrange from a few degrees below normal in some locations west of\nthe mtns to around 5 degrees above normal in the low deserts.\n\nFor Thursday through next weekend...The high pressure center \nwill shift to the southwest and build over the Desert Southwest. \nThis will bring significantly warmer conditions to SoCal, we are\nlikely to see the highest temperatures so far this summer. Models\nindicate that Saturday will likely be the hottest day, with\ndaytime temperatures ranging from 5-10 degrees above normal in the\ncoastal areas to about 15 degrees above normal in parts of the\ninland valleys and deserts. Temperatures will range from the high\n70s to the mid 80s near the coast, the low 90s to low triple\ndigits in inland OC and SD County, low triple digits to about 110\nin the IE, 80s to low 90 in the mtns, up to 113 in the high \ndeserts and up to around 120 in the low deserts. The building high\npressure will also make the marine layer shallower and restrict\nthe low clouds to the immediate coast during the nights and\nmornings. The heat will likely abate slightly for next Monday as \nthe high pressure weakens. Confidence in the coming high \ntemperatures is relatively high due to the stable pattern and good\nagreement among model solutions.",
